; Constructor refusals are explicitly unwrapped; failure edges clean the live prefix.
; CoreIR sample, family: cleanup (Drop, drop flags with UpdateMask, replace with
; install-before-drop by Copy, Box recursion, a variant made by set_case). FORM v1a
; and v1b, DROP-2, FAIL-8, FAIL-13.
;
; Source (package samples@0, module cleanup):
;   i0  resource Res { id: u64 }                       ; no finalizer: mechanical cleanup
;       type Pair { a: Res, b: Res }
;       enum Node { Nil, Cons(r: Res, next: Box[Node]) }
;       fn pair(x: u64, y: u64) { let p = Pair { a: mk(x), b: mk(y) } }
;       fn flags(take: bool) { let a = mk(1); let b = mk(2); if take { consume(a) } }
;       fn replace(x: u64, y: u64, z: u64) { let mut p = Pair { a: mk(x), b: mk(y) }; p.a = mk(z) }
;       fn chain(n: u64) { let mut l = Nil; for i in 1..=n { l = Cons(mk(i), Box(l)) } }
;       type Opened { f: HostFile, r: Res }
;       fn opened(f: HostFile, x: u64) { let o = Opened { f, r: mk(x) } }
;       type Table { m: Map[u64, Res] }
;       fn table(full: bool) { let t = Table { m: if full { {1: mk(10), 2: mk(20), 3: mk(30)}
;                                                          then remove(2), dropping mk(20) }
;                                                  else { map.new() } } }
;
; A resource is a pointer to its block; Res is charged 40 (storage 8, cell 32). f0 is
; `mk`, which unwraps a construction refusal. Ordinary callers here run without a
; limit, so `mk` is never refused; admission.core
; shows a refused allocation, which is an error value, never a failure (FAIL-2).
; Fields are cleaned in reverse order and owners in reverse declaration order
; (DROP-2). Each failure successor cleans exactly the owners live at that call.
; Drops of Res name no successor: mechanical cleanup never fails (FAIL-13).
;
; Behaviour, derived by hand (trace: Res ids in the order their cleanup started):
; run: f1(10, 11) -> | used 0 | trace 11, 10          ; Pair fields: b, then a
; run: f2(false) -> | used 0 | trace 2, 1             ; b, then a (its flag is set)
; run: f2(true) -> | used 0 | trace 1, 2              ; consume drops a; flag cleared
; run: f4(1, 2, 3) -> | used 0 | trace 1, 2, 3        ; old a at the replace, then b, a
; run: f5(3) -> | used 0 | trace 1, 2, 3              ; next before r: innermost first
; run: f5(0) -> | used 0                              ; Nil owns nothing
; A HostFile is a u64 provider handle, not a block: its drop closes it through the
; provider and ignores the outcome, admitting nothing (core.md, MEM-24); the trace
; records the close by the handle.
; run: f6(7, 10) -> | used 0 | trace 10, 7            ; r, then f: reverse order
; A Map carrier is {entries, slots, capacity}; an entry of Map[u64, Res] is hash (0), state
; (8: 1 live, 0 removed), key (16), value (24), stride 32 (layout `map_entry`). f7 builds
; the state after the inserts and the remove: three slots, the second removed (its key and
; value already cleaned by `map.remove`, its value word left 0). The drop cleans the live
; entries in insertion order, each key before its value, skips the removed one, then frees
; the entries (3 × 32 charged); an empty map (capacity 0) has no storage:
; run: f7(true) -> | used 0 | trace 10, 30
; run: f7(false) -> | used 0
core v1
